sections.body:
In lesson 3 of this track, `count++` lost updates because two threads could run its read and its write at the same moment. A transfer that does `a -= cents` and then `b += cents` has the same problem, and the warm-up showed a second one: a reader that looks between the two lines. Both go away if only one thread at a time can run the code that touches the balances.

Every Java object has a built-in lock called its **monitor** (or intrinsic lock). `synchronized` takes it on the way in and releases it on the way out, even if the block throws.

```java
private final Object lock = new Object();

void transfer(long cents) {
    synchronized (lock) {     // take lock's monitor, or wait until it is free
        a -= cents;
        b += cents;
    }                         // release it
}
```

```kotlin
private val lock = Any()

fun transfer(cents: Long) {
    synchronized(lock) {      // take lock's monitor, or wait until it is free
        a -= cents
        b += cents
    }                         // release it
}
```

A `synchronized` instance method is shorthand for `synchronized (this)` around the whole body. A `static synchronized` method locks the `Class` object, such as `Ledger.class`.

You get two guarantees, and you need both.

**Mutual exclusion.** While one thread holds a monitor, any other thread that tries to enter a block guarded by the same monitor waits, in the BLOCKED state from lesson 2 of this track. So the debit and the credit happen as one step, from the point of view of everyone else who takes that lock.

**Visibility.** When a thread releases a monitor, everything it wrote before that becomes visible to the next thread that takes the same monitor. The memory model calls this a happens-before edge from the release to the next lock. Without it, a thread could keep reading an old balance from a register or a CPU cache.

Both guarantees work only between threads that use the same monitor. A thread that reads `a` and `b` with no lock is not excluded and gets no visibility promise. It can read `a` after the debit and `b` before the credit, and report a total that is short by the amount in flight. Two auditors in the simulator below show the difference. Step T1 halfway through its transfer, then let T2 read. Then try the same trick on T3.

code.src:
package workbench.concurrency.l04;

/** A transfer thread moves money under a lock. An auditor adds up the two balances, with or without it. */
public class AuditDemo {
    static final Object lock = new Object();
    // volatile only so the JIT re-reads the fields on every pass; it does not make the pair consistent
    static volatile long a = 1_000, b = 1_000;

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        boolean auditorLocks = args.length > 0 && args[0].equals("locked");
        Thread transfers = new Thread(() -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 200_000; i++) {
                synchronized (lock) {        // debit and credit as one step
                    a -= 50;
                    b += 50;
                }
                synchronized (lock) {        // and move it back
                    a += 50;
                    b -= 50;
                }
            }
        });
        Thread auditor = new Thread(() -> {
            long audits = 0, wrong = 0;
            while (transfers.isAlive()) {
                long total;
                if (auditorLocks) {
                    synchronized (lock) { total = a + b; }
                } else {
                    total = a + b;           // reads a, then b, while transfers may run in between
                }
                audits++;
                if (total != 2_000) wrong++;
            }
            System.out.printf("auditor %s the lock: %,d audits, %,d saw a total other than 2,000%n",
                    auditorLocks ? "took" : "skipped", audits, wrong);
        });
        transfers.start();
        auditor.start();
        transfers.join();
        auditor.join();
    }
}

